A resource service validates tokens like this. The code, the provider and both services are written for this course.

Code sample:

  function validate(token) {
    const [body, mac] = token.split(".");
    if (!macOk(body, mac)) return "bad signature";     // verified against the provider key
    const c = claims(body);
    if (c.exp < NOW) return "expired";                // lifetime checked
    return { accepted: true, audience: c.aud, subject: c.sub };
  }

  // output from the reports service, for a request it should have refused
  { accepted: true, audience: 'api://orders', subject: 'svc-b' }

The signature is genuine and the token is not expired. Why was it accepted, and what is the fix?

A bucket policy holds one statement, written for this course.

Policy sample:

  {
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [ {
      "Sid": "share-reports",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": [ "s3:GetObject" ],
      "Resource": [ "arn:...:reports/2026/*" ]
    } ]
  }

The team believes the bucket is private apart from one folder. Reading the statement carefully, what has been made public, and what has not?

Objects are sealed as below. The key-management service, the store and the code are written for this course.

Code sample:

  function seal(objectId, plain) {
    const dek = randomBytes(32);                       // a fresh key per object
    const body  = aesGcm(plain, dek);                 // object bytes under the key
    const wrapIv = randomBytes(12);
    const wrapped = aesGcm(dek, wrappingKey, wrapIv); // key sealed under the wrapping key
    return { body, wrapped, wrapIv };
  }

Measured on a store of 2000 objects averaging 4096 bytes, replacing the wrapping key rewrote 64000 bytes in total. What does each layer encrypt, and what does that ratio explain?

A network policy is evaluated by this function, written for this course. It was run against three source addresses.

Code sample:

  const rules = [
    { src: "10.0.0.0/8",  action: "allow", what: "internal workloads" },
    { src: "10.1.0.0/16", action: "deny",  what: "the payroll subnet" },
  ];

  function evaluate(src) {
    for (const r of rules) if (inNet(src, r.src)) return r.action;
    return "allow";                 // <-- nothing below this line changes that
  }

Observed:

  10.2.3.4    -> allow
  10.1.0.9    -> allow
  203.0.113.9 -> allow

What is the defect, and what change makes the policy say what it appears to say?

A service mints a signed link for a report, written for this course.

Code sample:

  function mintLink(objectId, hours) {
    const exp = Date.now() + hours * 3600_000;
    const sig = hmac(secret, `${objectId}:${exp}`);       // signed, never verified by us
    return `https://files.example/${objectId}?e=${exp}&s=${sig}`;
  }

  app.get("/share/:id", (req, res) => {
    res.json({ link: mintLink(req.params.id, 24) });     // any caller, any object
  });

The design is described internally as a temporary share link. What is the actual exposure?
